idation pass rate) and produce machine-readable outputs plus a human-readable report.\n\n2. SCOPE, DEFINITIONS, AND THE \"BASIC\" PROPERTY SET\n\"Basic molecular properties\" = the computational set derivable from a 2D connection table (no 3D coordinates, no experimental measurements). The proposed set, with RDKit extraction functions, is:\n- Canonical SMILES (rdkit.Chem.MolToSmiles), InChI and InChIKey (rdkit.Chem.inchi.MolToInchi / MolToInchiKey) \u2014 identifiers and normalization.\n- Molecular formula (rdMolDescriptors.CalcMolFormula), exact mass (CalcExactMolWt), average MW (CalcMolWt).\n- Counts: heavy atoms (CalcNumHeavyAtoms), rotatable bonds (CalcNumRotatableBonds), H-bond donors (CalcNumHBD), H-bond acceptors (CalcNumHBA), rings (CalcNumRings), aromatic rings (CalcNumAromaticRings), heteroatoms (CalcNumHeteroatoms).\n- Physicochemical: TPSA (CalcTPSA), Crippen LogP and molar refractivity (CalcCrippenDescriptors), fraction sp3 (CalcFractionCSP3), formal charge (Chem.GetFormalCharge).\nDataset representation: a CSV/TSV with a header, a SMILES column, and an optional id column (auto-detected delimiter; configurable column names). No coordinates assumed.\n\n3. PROCEDURE PER STAGE\n\nStage A \u2014 Dataset & schema definition. Define an input contract (path, format, delimiter, SMILES column name, id column optional). Preflight with pandas: verify SMILES column exists, record row count and columns, reject empty/all-blank SMILES columns. Provide a small fixed validation fixture (~10\u201320 hand-written structures spanning functional groups, aromatic systems, stereocenters, and a charged species) used only for validation. The real dataset is user-supplied (e.g., a ChEMBL/PubChem-style subset); its schema is asserted at preflight and recorded in run_config.yaml.\n\nStage B \u2014 Extraction. For each input row: strip whitespace \u2192 parse with Chem.MolFromSmiles \u2192 canonicalize with Chem.MolToSmiles \u2192 compute all descriptors in the set \u2192 record parse_status plus the original and canonical SMILES for traceability. All descriptor computations are deterministic (no RNG involved), so results are bit-reproducible given a pinned tool version. Process in independent chunks; write checkpoint files (parquet) incrementally every chunk (e.g., 100k rows) so interrupted runs resume from the stored processed-index set. Parallelize with a multiprocessing Pool (workers = min(available_cores, 64), chunksize-based), then reassemble in original input order to keep output ordering identical to input ordering.\n\nStage C \u2014 Controls and validation (includes the fallback policy).\n1. Golden-set check: run the extractor on the fixed validation fixture; compare against trusted reference values (chemistry-rule-derived or a trusted reference such as PubChem constant tables). Acceptance: exact match for integer/string fields; \u22641e-4 relative error for float fields (MW, TPSA, LogP, MR). Any violation blocks further analysis until resolved.\n2. Round-trip canonicalization: canonical SMILES must re-parse and re-canonicalize identically; any failure is flagged as a parser edge case.\n3. Cross-tool spot check: sample ~1\u20132% (min 100) molecules and recompute a subset (formula, MW, heavy atoms, HBD/HBA, rings) with Open Babel; report the maximum discrepancy. Values above tolerance (count mismatches or >1e-4 on floats) are investigated and the resolution documented.\n4. Fallback policy for unsupported properties: for any property in the basic set that the pinned primary tool (RDKit) version does not support, the explicit fallback chain is (i) an equivalent RDKit function/pathway if one exists; (ii) the corresponding Open Babel implementation with the definitional difference documented; (iii) otherwise an explicit \"not_computed\" marker in the output \u2014 never a silent imputation. The QC report lists which fallback was taken and why.\n5. Identity/duplicate check: compute InChIKey per molecule; report unique-vs-total counts; duplicates are flagged and recorded (not auto-removed) so downstream analyses can optionally deduplicate.\n6. Parse-failure tracking: store failed input SMILES with error text; coverage = successful/total; if coverage < 98%, attempt an Open Babel parse of the failures before deciding, and report both coverage values.\n7. Sanity/range checks: non-negative integer counts, TPSA \u2265 0, MW within plausible small-molecule bounds, no NaN in numeric descriptors. Flags are recorded, never auto-corrected.\n8. Determinism check: run a small slice twice, once in reversed input order; assert bit-identical numeric outputs to rule out order-dependent behavior.\n\nStage D \u2014 Analysis plan. On the validated property matrix: (i) descriptive statistics per numeric descriptor (n, mean, median, std, min, quartiles, max, missing count) and unique counts for string fields; (ii) per-descriptor histograms, log-scaled where distributions are heavy-tailed; (iii) Pearson and Spearman correlation matrices with heatmaps \u2014 the expectation, to be reported and interpreted if observed, is strong positive correlation among structurally redundant descriptors (e.g., MW vs heavy atoms), and moderate correlations of TPSA with HBA and of rotatable bonds with MW; (iv) PCA on z-scored numeric descriptors (constant/perfectly collinear columns dropped first), reporting explained-variance ratios, loadings, and 2D projections of the first two PCs to characterize latent \"size\" vs \"polarity\" structure; (v) optional subgroup comparisons (e.g., Kruskal\u2013Wallis) only if a series/scaffold grouping column is present in the schema. No supervised/ML modeling is included; none is claimed.\n\nStage E \u2014 Feasibility, scale, and reproducibility.\n- Scale estimates (order-of-magnitude planning only, not measurements): RDKit typically parses ~1k\u201310k drug-like SMILES/sec per core with descriptor overhead; at up to 224 available cores (recommended worker cap 64), the pipeline is planned to sustain roughly tens to hundreds of thousands of molecules per minute; a million-molecule dataset is expected to complete in tens of minutes to a couple of hours. Runs batch at 100k rows with parquet checkpoints so any interruption resumes without recomputing.\n- Memory: the property matrix is small (~17 scalar fields per molecule); a million-molecule matrix is a few hundred MB; very large inputs are streamed in chunks.\n- Dependency/installation plan: required packages are rdkit, pandas, numpy, scipy, matplotlib, scikit-learn (pyarrow and openbabel optional). The current sandbox cannot satisfy these at runtime (network disabled \u2014 verified DNS/URL failure; Python 3.14.4; none of these imports resolve). A real run therefore requires: (a) pre-vendored offline wheels/conda packages, or (b) an explicit installation step in a networked/provisioned environment, plus (c) confirmation that RDKit wheels exist for the chosen Python (if not available for 3.14, provision a compatible interpreter such as 3.10\u20133.12 or pre-built binaries). All package versions and hashes are pinned; config, thresholds, and tool version strings are recorded in run_config.yaml and embedded in output metadata for reproducibility.\n\n4. EXPECTED OUTPUTS (of a future run \u2014 none have been produced)\n- properties.parquet / properties.csv \u2014 one row per input molecule (original+canonical SMILES, InChI, InChIKey, formula, all numeric properties, parse_status, error text if any).\n- qc_report.json + qc_report.md \u2014 coverage, golden-set pass/fail, round-trip pass rate, cross-tool max discrepancies, duplicate count, out-of-range flags, fallback usage notes.\n- descriptive_stats.csv; correlation_pearson.csv; correlation_spearman.csv; pca_summary.csv (variance + loadings); pca_projection.csv.\n- Figures: per-descriptor histograms, correlation heatmaps, PCA scatter plots.\n- run_config.yaml and a source tree (extractor, analyzer, runner scripts) sufficient to rerun end-to-end.\n\n5. HOW THE DESIGN ANS